# win32u: Avoid taking the user lock when calling window surface functions in update_surface_region().

Fix a regression that can cause a deadlock from "1a1d1f3f winemac.drv: Hide client_view when flushing window surfaces. Fix Amid Evil (SteamID: 673130) occasional freeze at startup.

The deadlock can happen in the following steps:
Thread A:
1. NtUserCreateWindowEx()->apply_window_pos()->macdrv_WindowPosChanged()->get_win_data(), which takes win_data_mutex.
2. Then macdrv_WindowPosChanged()->set_cocoa_window_properties()->NtUserGetWindowLongW()-> get_window_long_size()->get_win_ptr()->get_user_handle_ptr(), which blocks when trying to take the user lock.

Thread B:
1. NtUserSetLayeredWindowAttributes()->update_window_state()->apply_window_pos()->get_win_ptr()->get_user_handle_ptr(), which takes the user lock.
2. Then apply_window_pos()->update_surface_region()->window_surface_set_shape()->window_surface_flush()->macdrv_surface_flush()->get_win_data(), which blocks when trying to take win_data_mutex.

So we should avoid taking the user lock when calling window surface functions, similar to expose_window_surface(). update_surface_region() also has to be moved after release_win_ptr() in apply_window_pos() so that the user lock is not held when it's called.
